This is a familiar site to everyone with a conspiracy-inclined uncle.
In big, bold text It reads: "I want to believe." Below a blurry picture of a UFO. The surrounding woods and
off tilt angle give reason for the poor photography. This image exists
not as a function of photography, but despite photography, in fact,
the photo really doesn't matter. The image itself only represents the
true subject of the poster. This subject does not require photo
evidence, even belief doesn't really matter, what matters is the desire to
belief. So what is this subject?

This is an incredibly weird, vivid quote to me. It comes from a place of
clarity that is rarely found in this type of thinking. The act of
believing it framed as a virtue that exists despite
truth. It's almost like the religious virtue of faith, faith as a
substance of will. The only difference is the direction of the faith
doesn't matter. This is why it is perfect for the former religious
mind. It's saying the quiet part out loud.
Karl Popper said, *"the conspiracy theory
of society comes from abandoning God
and then asking: 'Who is in his place?'"* [^1]
The skeptic voices their complains and the conspiracist replies:
"yes, yes. I know. But you aren't getting the point. Nothing causes my
belief. To me, things start with belief and move upward."


The poster is associated with the X-Files. It’s a phrase that the
character Mulder says. This characters motivation is finding his lost
sister who was taken by aliens or something. I’ve never seen the
show. I bring this up to point out how much of an aesthetic thing this
is. Mulder’s obsessive research will lead to a worthwhile goal, this
is why he wants to believe. What then is the motivation for someone in
real life?

existential dread, replace religion.

When the conspiracy theorist finds the hidden CIA cameras, busts down
the door of a hidden attic, and finds his entire life being controlled
from a terminal, he is not filled with fear. His worst nightmare has
not come true. In fact, it’s the opposite!  He is filled with relief!
He was right! He wasn’t crazy. [^2]

This is (probably) the simplest logical statement:
```
p -> Q
if p
then Q
```
For the conspiracy theorist the arrow (->) is the justification.
It’s more about the connections than the thing’s that are being
connected. Connections and connections alone.

